.\"
.\" Manual page of "mcp-python" - MCP server, which runs a Python 3 code
.\" at a host.
.\"
.\" View the source: man ./src/mcp-python.8
.TH MCP-PYTHON 8 2026-11-14 "mcp-python 0.0.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mcp-python \- MCP server, which runs a Python 3 code at the host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B systemctl
.RB { start | stop | restart | status }
.B mcp-python
.PP
.B /var/lib/mcp-python/venv/bin/python /usr/lib/mcp-python/mcp-python.py
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mcp-python
is a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server over Streamable HTTP. A client
submits a Python 3 code, the server runs it in a fresh interpreter process
and returns the output. A client proves itself with a bearer token - see
AUTHENTICATION.
.PP
Each call is a separate process - no state between calls. The working
directory of a call is an empty temporary directory under
.BR MCP_SCRATCH ,
removed after the call. The code runs as
.B "python3 \-I \-c"
with the
.B MCP_WORK_PYTHON
interpreter;
.B \-I
isolates the Python import path only, it is not a security boundary.
.PP
The server takes no command line options - the configuration comes from
the environment. The Systemd unit
.B mcp-python.service
reads it from
.IR /etc/mcp-python/mcp-python.conf .
.PP
Started as root, the server reads the TLS certificate and key into memory,
then drops the privileges to
.B MCP_USER
(no supplementary groups) and only then serves. A submitted code runs as
.B MCP_USER
and can not read the key file. The server refuses to serve as root, if
.B MCP_USER
is not set.
.SH TOOLS
.TP
.BI run_python( code ", " timeout_s )
Runs
.I code
and returns
.BR returncode ,
.BR stdout ,
.B stderr
and
.BR timed_out .
.I timeout_s
(seconds, default 30) is capped by
.BR MCP_EXEC_TIMEOUT .
A code, which outlives the timeout, is killed. Standard input is
.IR /dev/null .
.TP
.B python_info()
Reports the version and the installed packages of the
.B MCP_WORK_PYTHON
interpreter.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
Each client has a token of its own and sends it with every request, in the
HTTP header
.BR "Authorization: Bearer <token>" .
A request without a known token is refused with the status
.BR 401 .
.PP
The clients are the files of the directory
.B MCP_AUTH_TOKENS
\- a file per client, the file name is the client name. A file keeps the
SHA-256 of the token, not the token: a submitted code may read the files
of the server, and here it finds nothing to connect with. The token itself
is shown once, when the client is added - it can not be shown again, only
replaced by a new one.
.PP
The files are managed with
.BR mcp-python-token (8)
\- the commands, the names and the format of a file are described there:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo mcp-python-token add laptop
sudo mcp-python-token list
sudo mcp-python-token remove laptop
.EE
.RE
.PP
The server reads the directory again by itself, at most once per 5
seconds
.RB ( MCP_AUTH_RELOAD_PERIOD )
- no restart: within that time a new client is accepted and a
removed one is refused, the sessions of the other clients stay. A session
belongs to the client, which opened it. The files with a leading dot are
skipped; a file with another content is skipped too, with a line in the
log.
.PP
With no clients every request is refused, and the log says so - it is the
state after the installation.
.B MCP_AUTH=none
turns the authentication off.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MCP_HOST
Listen address. Default
.BR 127.0.0.1 .
.TP
.B MCP_HTTP_VARIANT
Which listeners to start -
.BR HTTP ,
.B HTTPS
or
.BR both .
Default
.BR HTTP .
.TP
.B MCP_PORT
HTTP listen port. Default
.BR 8765 .
.TP
.B MCP_PORT_HTTPS
HTTPS listen port. Default
.BR 8766 .
.TP
.BR MCP_TLS_CERT ", " MCP_TLS_KEY
TLS certificate and key (PEM), for HTTPS.
.TP
.B MCP_USER
Account to serve as, and so to run a submitted code as. The package
creates the system account
.BR mcp-python .
.TP
.B MCP_WORK_PYTHON
Interpreter for a submitted code, e.g. of a virtual environment with the
packages a client needs. Default
.BR python3 ;
the package configuration sets
.IR /usr/bin/python3 .
.TP
.B MCP_SCRATCH
Directory for the per call temporary directories; must be writable by
.BR MCP_USER .
Default
.IR /tmp ;
the package configuration sets
.IR /var/lib/mcp-python/scratch .
.TP
.B MCP_EXEC_TIMEOUT
Seconds, upper limit for a single
.B run_python
call. Default
.BR 60 .
.TP
.B MCP_OUTPUT_LIMIT
Characters of
.B stdout
and
.B stderr
(each) returned to a client; the rest is cut. Default
.BR 65536 .
.TP
.B MCP_AUTH
Authentication of the clients -
.B token
or
.BR none .
Default
.BR token .
.TP
.B MCP_AUTH_TOKENS
Directory of the token files, a file per client; must be readable by
.BR MCP_USER ,
unless
.B MCP_AUTH_RELOAD_PERIOD
is
.BR once .
Default
.IR /etc/mcp-python/tokens .
.TP
.B MCP_AUTH_RELOAD_PERIOD
Seconds between the reads of
.BR MCP_AUTH_TOKENS ,
or
.BR once :
the files are read at start, by root, before the privileges drop, and
never again - they may be readable by root only, so a submitted code can
not read even the hashes; a change of the clients needs a restart. An
unreadable directory stops the start. Default
.BR 5 .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/mcp-python/mcp-python.conf
Configuration - environment of the unit. After a change:
.BR "systemctl restart mcp-python" .
.TP
.I /etc/mcp-python/tokens
The clients - see AUTHENTICATION. The directory is of root, readable by
the group
.BR mcp-python .
Kept at the package removal; deleted at the purge - the files are
overwritten with
.BR shred (1)
first, if it is installed.
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/mcp-python-token
The clients management - see
.BR mcp-python-token (8).
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/mcp-python.service
The Systemd unit. Hardening:
.BR ProtectSystem=strict ,
.BR ProtectHome=yes ,
.BR PrivateTmp=yes ,
.BR PrivateDevices=yes ,
.BR MemoryMax=1G ,
.B TasksMax=64
and more - only
.I /var/lib/mcp-python/scratch
is writable for the service.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/mcp-python/mcp-python.py
The server launcher - listeners, TLS, privileges drop.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/mcp-python/mcp_tools.py
The MCP tools.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/mcp-python/mcp_auth.py
The authentication - and
.B mcp-python-token
itself.
.TP
.I /var/lib/mcp-python/venv
Virtual environment of the server - created at the package configuration,
with the host
.BR python3 ,
from the Python package index pip is configured for. Removed at the
package removal.
.TP
.IR /usr/share/mcp-python/requirements.txt ", " /usr/share/mcp-python/requirements.lock
Packages of the server virtual environment, and their exact set of the
build reference (constraints).
.TP
.I /var/lib/mcp-python/scratch
Per call temporary directories.
.TP
.I /var/log/mcp-python/venv-create.log
Log of the last virtual environment creation;
.I venv-create.log.1
\- of the one before it.
.SH SECURITY
A client with a valid token executes a code as
.BR MCP_USER .
The authentication decides, who gets in - it does not separate the clients
from each other: every token gives the same, full code execution.
.PP
The network, the processes and the other clients' calls are not limited -
a code has everything
.B MCP_USER
has, minus what the Systemd unit takes away.
.PP
A token over plain HTTP is readable on the wire. Beyond the local host use
the HTTPS listener, or TLS in front of the server; the server logs a
warning for an HTTP listener at a non-loopback address. TLS alone protects
the traffic only - it is no authentication.
.PP
A token does not expire - it is valid until its file is removed. Never
pass a token to the server side through the environment - a submitted code
inherits it.
.PP
With
.B MCP_AUTH=none
any host, which reaches a listener, executes a code. With the default
.B MCP_HOST=127.0.0.1
it is every local user and process of the host.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start the server and follow its log:
.PP
.RS
.EX
systemctl enable \-\-now mcp-python
journalctl \-u mcp-python \-f
.EE
.RE
.PP
Add a client, then connect Claude Code to a local server with the token
printed:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo mcp-python-token add laptop
claude mcp add \-\-transport http python3 http://127.0.0.1:8765/mcp \e
    \-\-header "Authorization: Bearer <token>"
.EE
.RE
.SH CONTAINER
The container image has the file tree of the package, the account
.B mcp-python
and the server virtual environment, created at the image build. There is
no Systemd - the server is the process of the container, started as
.BR mcp-python ,
and the configuration is the environment of the container
.RB ( \-\-env ", " \-\-env-file ).
.I /etc/mcp-python/mcp-python.conf
of the image is a reference, it is not read. The image sets
.BR MCP_HOST=0.0.0.0 ,
.BR MCP_HTTP_VARIANT=HTTP ,
.BR MCP_USER=mcp-python ,
.B MCP_WORK_PYTHON=/usr/local/bin/python3
and
.BR MCP_SCRATCH=/var/lib/mcp-python/scratch .
.PP
The image has no clients - the token files come from outside, as a
directory
.RI ( <tokens-dir>
below) mounted read only at
.IR /etc/mcp-python/tokens ,
the default of
.BR MCP_AUTH_TOKENS .
They have to be readable by the account of the container, as the
ones made by
.B mcp-python-token
are. At a host without the package - from the source tree:
.PP
.RS
.EX
python3 src/mcp_auth.py \-\-dir <tokens-dir> add laptop
.EE
.RE
.PP
Never a token in
.B \-\-env
\- see SECURITY. In Kubernetes the same directory is a Secret, a key per
client, mounted as a volume -
.B auth.secretName
of the Helm chart. A client is added or revoked by applying the Secret
again: the files of the pods follow in about a minute, no restart. Without
the Secret there are no clients - every request is refused.
.PP
Build the image (from the source tree) and launch it, with the limits the
Systemd unit has - only the scratch is writable:
.PP
.RS
.EX
make container
docker run \-\-rm \-\-init \-\-read-only \e
    \-\-tmpfs /var/lib/mcp-python/scratch:mode=1777 \e
    \-\-cap-drop ALL \-\-security-opt no-new-privileges \e
    \-\-memory 1g \-\-pids-limit 64 \e
    \-\-volume <tokens-dir>:/etc/mcp-python/tokens:ro \e
    \-\-publish 127.0.0.1:8765:8765 \e
    mcp-python
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B make container-launch
does it by
.BR mcp-python-container (1),
with the client
.B dev
of the source tree - the secrets in
.IR build/mcp-python
(the token -
.IR build/mcp-python/clients/dev.token ),
HTTP and HTTPS.
Which host addresses reach the listener is up to
.B \-\-publish
- see SECURITY.
.PP
HTTPS - start as root, with the two capabilities the privileges drop
needs; the server reads the key, then drops to
.B MCP_USER
itself, as at a host. The key file has to be owned by root - without
other capabilities root reads only its own files:
.PP
.RS
.EX
docker run \-\-rm \-\-init \-\-read-only \e
    \-\-tmpfs /var/lib/mcp-python/scratch:mode=1777 \e
    \-\-user root \-\-cap-drop ALL \-\-cap-add SETUID \-\-cap-add SETGID \e
    \-\-security-opt no-new-privileges \e
    \-\-volume <tokens-dir>:/etc/mcp-python/tokens:ro \e
    \-\-volume /etc/ssl/certs/mcp-python.crt:/run/tls.crt:ro \e
    \-\-volume /etc/ssl/private/mcp-python.key:/run/tls.key:ro \e
    \-\-env MCP_HTTP_VARIANT=HTTPS \e
    \-\-env MCP_TLS_CERT=/run/tls.crt \-\-env MCP_TLS_KEY=/run/tls.key \e
    \-\-publish 127.0.0.1:8766:8766 \e
    mcp-python
.EE
.RE
.PP
Packages for a submitted code - an image on top, with them installed for
the
.B MCP_WORK_PYTHON
interpreter:
.PP
.RS
.EX
FROM mcp-python
USER root
RUN /usr/local/bin/python3 \-m pip install \-\-no-cache-dir numpy
USER mcp-python
.EE
.RE
.SH HTTPS WITH A SELF-SIGNED CERTIFICATE
An agent (an MCP client - Claude Code, Claude Desktop, MCP Inspector, a
script) reaches the server over Streamable HTTP at the path
.IR /mcp .
Over HTTPS with a self-signed certificate two conditions must hold at the
agent side:
.IP 1. 4
The host part of the URL - a name or an address, by which the agent
reaches the server - is in the Subject Alternative Name of the
certificate: a name as
.BR DNS: ,
an address as
.BR IP: .
Otherwise the host name check of TLS fails. The Common Name of the
certificate is not checked by current clients. A host with several names
or addresses is reachable over HTTPS by those of them only, which the
certificate lists.
.IP 2. 4
The agent trusts the certificate. A self-signed certificate is its own
CA, so it must be added to the trust store the agent uses - and not every
runtime uses the OS store.
.PP
Names used below:
.TP 14
.I <host>
A name or an address, by which the agent reaches the server; listed in
the Subject Alternative Name of the certificate.
.TP
.I <cert-file>
A copy of the certificate (PEM, public part) at the agent machine.
.TP
.I <port-https>
.BR MCP_PORT_HTTPS ,
default
.BR 8766 .
.TP
.I <server-name>
Any name, by which the agent knows the server.
.TP
.I <token>
The token of the agent, as printed by
.B mcp-python-token add
\- see AUTHENTICATION.
.PP
The endpoint:
.BR https://<host>:<port-https>/mcp .
.SH HTTPS - SERVER SIDE
In
.IR /etc/mcp-python/mcp-python.conf :
.PP
.RS
.EX
MCP_HOST=0.0.0.0
MCP_HTTP_VARIANT=HTTPS
MCP_PORT_HTTPS=8766
MCP_TLS_CERT=/etc/ssl/certs/mcp-python.crt
MCP_TLS_KEY=/etc/ssl/private/mcp-python.key
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B MCP_HTTP_VARIANT=both
keeps the HTTP listener too. The key may stay readable by root only - the
server reads it before the privileges drop. Then:
.B "systemctl restart mcp-python"
\- a reissued certificate also needs a restart.
.PP
How the certificate is issued is out of scope of the package. For a test,
a self-signed one - list in
.B subjectAltName
every name and address, by which agents are to reach the server, e.g.
.BR DNS:<host>,DNS:<other-name>,IP:<address> :
.PP
.RS
.EX
openssl req \-x509 \-newkey rsa:3072 \-sha256 \-days 825 \-nodes \e
    \-subj "/CN=<host>" \e
    \-addext "subjectAltName=DNS:<host>" \e
    \-keyout /etc/ssl/private/mcp-python.key \e
    \-out /etc/ssl/certs/mcp-python.crt
chmod 0600 /etc/ssl/private/mcp-python.key
.EE
.RE
.PP
Copy
.I /etc/ssl/certs/mcp-python.crt
(never the key) to the agent machine - it is
.IR <cert-file> .
.SH HTTPS - AGENT OS TRUST
Debian / Ubuntu, at the agent machine:
.IP 1. 4
Put
.I <cert-file>
into the OS local CA directory. The file must be PEM and its name must end
with
.B .crt
\- other files are ignored:
.RS
.PP
.EX
sudo install \-m 0644 <cert-file> /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/
.EE
.RE
.IP 2. 4
Rebuild the OS bundle
.I /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt
\- expect
.BR "1 added" :
.RS
.PP
.EX
sudo update-ca-certificates
.EE
.RE
.IP 3. 4
Check - an HTTP status, no TLS error, no
.BR \-k .
A
.B 405
on
.B HEAD
is normal - the endpoint takes GET, POST and DELETE. A
.B 401
\- the trust is fine, the token is not:
.RS
.PP
.EX
curl \-I \-H "Authorization: Bearer <token>" \e
    https://<host>:<port-https>/mcp
.EE
.RE
.PP
To remove the trust - delete the file from
.I /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/
and run
.BR "sudo update-ca-certificates \-\-fresh" .
.SH HTTPS - AGENT RUNTIME TRUST
The OS store is enough for
.BR curl (1)
and for the clients, which use it. But there are corner cases with OS
keystore ignore and own CA list list distribution - point
them to the OS bundle in their environment:
.TP
.B Python (httpx - mcp, mcp-proxy)
.BR SSL_CERT_FILE=/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt .
.TP
.B Python (requests)
.BR REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE=/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt .
.TP
.B Node.js (mcp-remote, MCP Inspector, Electron applications)
.BR NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS=/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt ,
or with Node.js 22.15+ / 23.8+
.BR NODE_OPTIONS=\-\-use\-system\-ca .
Packaged Electron applications may ignore
.BR NODE_OPTIONS ,
while
.B NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS
works there.

Also consider usage of
.BR NODE_USE_SYSTEM_CA=1 .
It works with, at least, certain other applications.

.PP
A variable is read once, at the process start. An application started
from a menu or a launcher does not see a variable of a shell - set it
there, or in the client configuration
.RB ( env
of a server entry). Quit the application completely (including its tray
icon) before a new start - otherwise the start only activates the old
process.
.SH HTTPS - CLAUDE CLIENTS
How hard it is to connect depends on the client - after the trust above,
Claude Code needs one command, Claude Desktop two steps, the Desktop custom
connector does not work at all.
.TP
.B Claude Code with TUI
It have a package, in a different Apt repository. It talks Streamable HTTP
itself and trusts the OS
store (checked with version 2.1.309) - no variable:
.RS
.PP
.EX
claude mcp add \-\-scope user \-\-transport http <server-name> \e
    https://<host>:<port-https>/mcp \e
    \-\-header "Authorization: Bearer <token>"
claude mcp list
.EE
.PP
Inside a session
.B /mcp
shows the server status and tools. Should a version fail with a TLS
error, start it with
.BR NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS ,
as above.
.RE
.TP
.B Project configuration (.mcp.json)
The same, without the command line. The file is usually shared - the
token comes from a variable of the environment Claude Code is started in:
.RS
.PP
.EX
{
    "mcpServers": {
        "<server-name>": {
            "type": "http",
            "url": "https://<host>:<port-https>/mcp",
            "headers": {
                "Authorization": "Bearer ${MCP_PYTHON_TOKEN}"
            }
        }
    }
}
.EE
.RE
.TP
.B Claude Desktop
Install a bridge, add a configuration entry. Its
local configuration accepts only a command, so it needs a local bridge, started by Desktop and talking to it over standard
input and output. With the Python
.B mcp-proxy
(PyPI, in a virtual environment of its own), in
.IR ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json :
.RS
.PP
.EX
"mcpServers": {
    "<server-name>": {
        "command": "<venv>/bin/mcp-proxy",
        "args": [
            "\-\-transport", "streamablehttp",
            "\-\-headers", "Authorization", "Bearer <token>",
            "https://<host>:<port-https>/mcp"
        ],
        "env": {
            "SSL_CERT_FILE": "/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt"
        }
    }
}
.EE
.PP
Or with the Node.js
.B mcp-remote
(Node.js 22.15+ / 23.8+, fetched by
.B npx
at each start; pin a version -
.B mcp-remote@<version>
\- for a repeatable behaviour):
.PP
.EX
"mcpServers": {
    "<server-name>": {
        "command": "npx",
        "args": [
            "\-y", "mcp-remote",
            "https://<host>:<port-https>/mcp",
            "\-\-header", "Authorization: Bearer <token>"
        ],
        "env": {
            "NODE_OPTIONS": "\-\-use\-system\-ca"
        }
    }
}
.EE
.PP
With an older Node.js - replace the
.B env
entry with
.BR "\(dqNODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS\(dq: \(dq/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt\(dq" .
.PP
.B command
must be an absolute path, or be on the
.B PATH
of Desktop.
.B mcp-proxy
defaults to SSE, hence
.BR "\-\-transport streamablehttp" .
Quit Desktop completely and start it again - the configuration is read
at start only.
.RE
.TP
.B Claude Desktop custom connector
Not usable for a private network. Such connectors (Settings, Connectors)
are reached from Anthropic's cloud, which can not resolve or reach a
private host name.
.TP
.B Python script (mcp 2.x)
Run with
.BR SSL_CERT_FILE :
.RS
.PP
.EX
import asyncio
from mcp import Client
from mcp.client.streamable_http import (
    create_mcp_http_client, streamable_http_client)

async def main():
    http = create_mcp_http_client(
        headers={"Authorization": "Bearer <token>"})
    url = "https://<host>:<port-https>/mcp"
    async with Client(streamable_http_client(url, http_client=http)) as c:
        r = await c.call_tool("run_python", {"code": "print(6 * 7)"})
        print(r.content[0].text)

asyncio.run(main())
.EE
.RE
.PP
Over plain HTTP no trust is needed, the token is - and it is readable on
the wire, see SECURITY: Claude Code takes the same command with an
.B http://
URL;
.B mcp-remote
refuses a non-localhost URL without
.BR \-\-allow\-http .
.PP
The
.B claude
command line client is not packaged by the authors and is not a
dependency of this package - as the authors' choice, it is to be
downloaded separately, from Anthropic. (Claude Desktop keeps its own
copies under
.IR ~/.config/Claude/claude-code/<version>/ ,
not on the
.BR PATH .)
.SH HTTPS - DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B 401 Unauthorized
No
.B Authorization
header, or its token is not known to the server - check the header at the
agent and
.B mcp-python-token list
at the server. A client just added is accepted within 5 seconds. With no
clients at all, or with a token file skipped, there is a line in
.BR "journalctl \-u mcp-python" .
.TP
.B SELF_SIGNED_CERT_IN_CHAIN, DEPTH_ZERO_SELF_SIGNED_CERT, unable to verify the first certificate
Node.js does not trust the certificate - set
.BR NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS .
Often shown by a client just as
.BR "Server unavailable" .
.TP
.B CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED: self-signed certificate
Python does not trust the certificate - set
.BR SSL_CERT_FILE .
.TP
.B Hostname mismatch, certificate is not valid for
The host part of the URL is not in the Subject Alternative Name of the
certificate - connect by a listed name or address, or reissue the
certificate with it; list them with
.BR "openssl x509 \-in <cert-file> \-noout \-ext subjectAltName" .
.TP
.B curl fails too
The OS trust is missing - see
.BR "HTTPS - AGENT OS TRUST" ;
or the server is not reached - check
.BR MCP_HOST ,
.B MCP_HTTP_VARIANT
and the port, and
.BR "journalctl \-u mcp-python" .
.TP
.B 404 Session not found
The server restarted while a client held a session - reconnect the
client. The same answer gets a session used with the token of another
client - a session belongs to the client, which opened it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mcp-python-token (8),
.BR systemctl (1),
